  "raw_text": "Step 1: Analyze the function $f(x) = |x - p| + |x - 15| + |x - p - 15|$ within the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$.\n\nStep 2: Since $0 < p < 15$, we know that $p < 15$. Therefore, within the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$, the term $|x - p - 15|$ will always be positive because $x - p - 15 < 0$.\n\nStep 3: Within the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$, the terms $|x - p|$ and $|x - 15|$ can be simplified to $x - p$ and $15 - x$ respectively, since both $x - p \\geq 0$ and $15 - x \\geq 0$ in this range.\n\nStep 4: Substitute these simplified expressions into $f(x)$ to get $f(x) = (x - p) + (15 - x) + |x - p - 15| = 15 - p + |x - p - 15|$.\n\nStep 5: Notice that when $p \\leq x \\leq 15$, the term $|x - p - 15|$ simplifies further to $15 - x - p$ because $x - p - 15 \\leq 0$.\n\nStep 6: Thus, $f(x) = 15 - p + (15 - x - p) = 30 - 2p - x$.\n\nStep 7: To find the minimum value of $f(x)$ in the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$, observe that $f(x)$ is a linear function decreasing with respect to $x$. The minimum value occurs at the maximum value of $x$ in the given interval, which is $x = 15$.\n\nStep 8: Substitute $x = 15$ into $f(x)$ to find the minimum value: $f(15) = 30 - 2p - 15 = 15 - 2p$.\n\nFinal answer: $\\boxed{15 - 2p}$<|im_end|>",
